        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: दुहिता",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "दुहिता (duhitā) — \"daughter\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Back to the -ṛ family: the stem is दुहितृ (duhitṛ-), standing beside पिता (pitā) and माता (mātā). Its cousins are English daughter, Greek thugatēr, Gothic dauhtar, Persian duxtar. Indian tradition read it as 'the one who milks', from √दुह् (duh) — a tidy story about a daughter's work in the house, which comparative linguists do not accept, because the cousins are older than the pun. Keep the story, and keep the fact that it is one."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "That closes the run: father, mother, son, daughter — the four words the family kept longest."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
